<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">probably the late 1860s (sometime after 1865)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Swing Low, Sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a>&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> Willis, Choctaw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> living in the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Steal Away&quot; is counted, with &quot;<b>Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</b>&quot;, &quot;Wade in the Water&quot; and &quot;The Gospel Train&quot;, among the spirituals said to carry hidden codes: besides their faith in God, messages telling enslaved people to run away, on their own or by the Underground Railroad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</b>&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter Minerva Willis, Choctaw Freedmen living in the old Indian Territory, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, Oklahoma.</blockquote><a href="/songs/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a></li></ul></section>
